            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has an excess amount of air leakage that can be addressed as you complete your Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program upgrades. The current air leakage locations include:



** The adjoining wall between the kitchen/laundry area and the ensuite bathroom



** The dryer vent, also located in the interior wall mentioned above



** Around the master bedroom door, as well as many of the older original windows



** Some of the wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls of the house



** Coming from a few of the furnace supply ducts in the floor - especially the one in the kitchen. The ductwork for the furnace is allowing air infiltration, which would not happen if the ductwork was fully sealed without an air leakage path to the exterior of the house (likely into the belly bag or crawlspace of the house).



Savings: I modeled a modest 25% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 163 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.5 GJ. For your house, you may even achieve greater reduction !!



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a 25% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 750 rebate. If you achieve a minimum 30% reduction - this rebate would increase to $1,000 !!



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I modeled adding R10 rigid foam board insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover most (perhaps all) of the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For semi-detached housing, the above rebates are adjusted to 75%.



Savings: By adding R10 to 100% of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 183 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,250 in rebates from the DHERP. You may decide to add more exterior insulation (perhaps R15) and this would increase the rebate to $3,750, however you will want to be sure the additional wall thickness (along the left side of your house) does not significantly affect the ventilation of your attic space !!



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls were originally uninsulated. While you were waiting for the Arctic Energy Alliance to come evaluate your house, you decided to proceed to start adding insulation to the interior of these walls in preparation for this current heating season. At the time of our visit, you had already added 1.5" (R7.5) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls ............ and you are planning to add a 2nd layer (another R7.5) to the interior.



Savings: By adding a total combined R15 of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 58 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 750 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Note: Adding R10 - R23 to 100% of the exterior walls and header space of a crawlspace qualifies for $1,000 for detached housing and 75% ($750) for semi-detached housing. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (8 + the 1 skylight) are older windows that could be upgraded in conjunction with your planned exterior wall insulation &amp; siding project. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 9 older windows (including the skylight) with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 186 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ $2,250 in rebates (9 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



Both of the exterior doors in this house are steel insulated doors with double pane glass windows. The doors are generally in good shape, but I recommend you replace the weather stripping around the interior of the frame to reduce draft, and increase comfort. This is especially important in the master bedroom.



Rebates: There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 50 USG electric hot water tank. We discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 1,207 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house has an older original wood fireplace that is not a fully sealed unit, and does allow heat loss from the house year-round even when not being used. The "triangle shaped space" it is installed into has cold walls as it is a closed space that incorporates the chimney venting for that unit. Now that you have a propane fuel supply in the house, I modeled the opportunity for you to have a propane fireplace insert installed - a direct vent, sealed combustion unit that has a spark ignition (not a continuous pilot light).



Savings:

Whether you have a wood fireplace, wood stove or a propane fireplace, these would all be "supplementary" heating systems to the main heating system - the 95% AFUE propane furnace you recently installed.



If you replace the current non-sealed wood fireplace with a propane fireplace (as outlined above), there is an estimated $46 of annual energy savings.



ANOTHER OPTION:

If you are interested in supplementing your heating with wood, you can choose to upgrade the old wood fireplace to a new EPA qualified wood stove that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 82% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $ 309 per year in energy costs. Installing an eligible wood stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $7,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $2,250 for the wall insulation

     - $   750 for the crawlspace wall insulation

     - $2,250 for replacing 9 windows with new eligible units

     - $   750 for a 25% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate (perhaps even a $2,000 bonus rebate)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the crawlspace wall insulation, however this would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - On-Demand Hot Water (minimum 0.91 UEF) - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the two bathroom fans and the dryer - the kitchen range hood was not yet installed), indicated a pressure drop of only 1.3 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is direct vent, however the wood fireplace is not a sealed unit &amp; is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan has been purchased, however was not yet installed

        - 2 bathroom fans (both very old &amp; weak)

        - An older clothes dryer (and you had a brand new one ready to be installed)



Overall, your house currently has excess air leakage (heat loss) and the renovations you have planned (and the information outlined in this report) will help you to reduce this air leakage - saving you energy &amp; money, while still maintaining an adequate indoor air quality.



I would recommend you replace both bathroom fans with new Energy Star rated units. You may want to consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



